tools, and so on.

00:10:42.860 --> 00:10:45.910
So getting observability--
and, by the way,

00:10:45.910 --> 00:10:47.830
I'm using the term
"observability" here.

00:10:47.830 --> 00:10:50.140
And I know it sort of became
kind of like a buzz word

00:10:50.140 --> 00:10:51.490
recently.

00:10:51.490 --> 00:10:54.787
But at Google, we truly mean--
and from many years ago.

00:10:54.787 --> 00:10:56.620
We've been talking more
about observability.

00:10:56.620 --> 00:10:57.661
It's not just monitoring.

00:10:57.661 --> 00:10:59.450
Monitoring is one
piece of things.

00:10:59.450 --> 00:11:01.840
Monitoring is just that
activity of going in and looking

00:11:01.840 --> 00:11:04.240
at specific things
to prevent failure.

00:11:04.240 --> 00:11:06.920
Observability is more
a property of a system.

00:11:06.920 --> 00:11:09.010
So from the beginning,
what we're trying to do

00:11:09.010 --> 00:11:11.710
is to get our systems
being designed

00:11:11.710 --> 00:11:13.540
with reliability in mind.

00:11:13.540 --> 00:11:15.640
It really is an
engineering practice.

00:11:15.640 --> 00:11:18.040
And so we're trying to
increase the signals.

00:11:18.040 --> 00:11:21.550
That's why you see metrics,
logs, traces all together,

00:11:21.550 --> 00:11:24.250
because we're trying to
increase the observability

00:11:24.250 --> 00:11:27.760
of our systems so we can
know how they are operating

00:11:27.760 --> 00:11:30.460
under normal circumstances
and also how they

00:11:30.460 --> 00:11:32.590
operate when there's failure.

00:11:32.590 --> 00:11:35.770
So that way we can quickly
resolve issues, speed-read cost

00:11:35.770 --> 00:11:40.740
analysis, and help us resolve
the issue and then [INAUDIBLE]..

00:11:40.740 --> 00:11:44.140
Kubernetes actually makes
things a little bit trickier,

00:11:44.140 --> 00:11:47.440
because, on the one hand, it
gives us so much flexibility.

00:11:47.440 --> 00:11:48.310
It's great.

00:11:48.310 --> 00:11:50.380
We start with new
microservices, so it really

00:11:50.380 --> 00:11:55.660
allows us to break this monolith
into multiple micro services.

00:11:55.660 --> 00:11:58.840
But that means that we have more
pieces to monitor and operate.

00:11:58.840 --> 00:12:00.520
The second thing is
that we, of course,

00:12:00.520 --> 00:12:02.190
have abstracted infrastructure.

00:12:02.190 --> 00:12:03.620
We have so much flexibility.

00:12:03.620 --> 00:12:07.360
You can use different types
of controllers and deployments

00:12:07.360 --> 00:12:09.940
and StatefulSets and all
these different components.

00:12:09.940 --> 00:12:12.400
And then, again, when
you're using those,

00:12:12.400 --> 00:12:15.220
now you have to infer the
state of those components

00:12:15.220 --> 00:12:16.730
when something goes wrong.

00:12:16.730 --> 00:12:20.200
So you really want to be able
to have pretty well-spread-out

00:12:20.200 --> 00:12:23.780
observability throughout your
entire Kubernetes environment.

00:12:23.780 --> 00:12:25.510
And finally,
Kubernetes is really

00:12:25.510 --> 00:12:27.340
a highly dynamic environment.

00:12:27.340 --> 00:12:30.652
If you're using auto-scaling
and, of course, the scheduling

00:12:30.652 --> 00:12:32.860
and restarting and so on,
that's a natural occurrence

00:12:32.860 --> 00:12:33.997
in Kubernetes.

00:12:33.997 --> 00:12:35.830
So you want to be able
to have a system that

00:12:35.830 --> 00:12:40.150
allows you to always know the
state of where things are,

00:12:40.150 --> 00:12:41.192
no matter where they are.

00:12:41.192 --> 00:12:42.566
And, of course,
you're not really

00:12:42.566 --> 00:12:44.500
looking at your
applications necessarily,

00:12:44.500 --> 00:12:46.594
like, knowing which
machine they are, right?

00:12:46.594 --> 00:12:47.260
That's the idea.

00:12:47.260 --> 00:12:49.670
We want you to not think
about any of those things.

00:12:49.670 --> 00:12:51.190
And so-- but that
means now that you

00:12:51.190 --> 00:12:53.860
need to keep track of
where your application is

00:12:53.860 --> 00:12:55.490
and what it's doing and so on.

00:12:55.490 --> 00:12:59.200
So what we have right
now is a new solution,

00:12:59.200 --> 00:13:00.970
which is Stackdriver
Kubernetes Monitoring

00:13:00.970 --> 00:13:02.094
that we announced recently.

00:13:02.094 --> 00:13:02.890
It's in beta.

00:13:02.890 --> 00:13:04.470
And we've been
expanding it rapidly.

00:13:04.470 --> 00:13:07.420
So we invite you-- all of
you-- to please start using it,

00:13:07.420 --> 00:13:08.560
if you haven't already.

00:13:08.560 --> 00:13:09.430
Give us feedback.

00:13:09.430 --> 00:13:11.200
The feedback that you
give us right now,

00:13:11.200 --> 00:13:14.420
we directly are
reading those messages.

00:13:14.420 --> 00:13:16.954
So we want to build a product
that is most useful to you.

00:13:16.954 --> 00:13:18.870
There's many things that
are not available yet

00:13:18.870 --> 00:13:20.411
in Stackdriver
Kubernetes Monitoring.

00:13:20.411 --> 00:13:23.750
We want to get those things
out to you as fast as possible.

00:13:23.750 --> 00:13:27.190
So let me go through it, very
rapidly, some of the things

00:13:27.190 --> 00:13:28.420
that we have.

00:13:28.420 --> 00:13:30.900
First, it's comprehensive
observability.

00:13:30.900 --> 00:13:32.270
What do I mean by that?

00:13:32.270 --> 00:13:35.140
Out of the box, without
you having to do anything,

00:13:35.140 --> 00:13:40.180
you get all this instrumentation
done for free, so to speak.

00:13:40.180 --> 00:13:43.832
You get instrumentation on
all your Kubernetes objects.

00:13:43.832 --> 00:13:45.040
And you get not just metrics.

00:13:45.040 --> 00:13:48.400
You get metrics, logs, and
we have traces as well.

00:13:48.400 --> 00:13:50.890
And there's events and
metadata and so on-- so really

00:13:50.890 --> 00:13:52.240
trying to increase that.

00:13:52.240 --> 00:13:54.340
It works very well
with open source.

00:13:54.340 --> 00:13:56.710
From the ground up, we
built it and integrated it

00:13:56.710 --> 00:13:58.790
with Prometheus,
which is, of course,

00:13:58.790 --> 00:14:01.240
the most well-known
monitoring open source

00:14:01.240 --> 00:14:03.130
solution for Kubernetes.

00:14:03.130 --> 00:14:04.750
It's multi-cluster
monitoring, which

00:14:04.750 --> 00:14:06.550
is a difference that you
see from many other products

00:14:06.550 --> 00:14:07.222
outside.

00:14:07.222 --> 00:14:08.680
In a single place,
we're giving you

00:14:08.680 --> 00:14:12.110
a way to do multiple monitoring
of multiple clusters.

00:14:12.110 --> 00:14:14.020
And it doesn't
matter where it runs.

00:14:14.020 --> 00:14:16.480
We can monitor your cluster
on-prem or your cluster

00:14:16.480 --> 00:14:21.310
on Kubernetes engine or your
cluster running on AWS EC2

00:14:21.310 --> 00:14:24.572
instances, for instance.

00:14:24.572 --> 00:14:26.530
One last thing that I
would say in this respect

00:14:26.530 --> 00:14:30.100
is the fact that if you have
the observability no matter what

00:14:30.100 --> 00:14:31.150
your role is.

00:14:31.150 --> 00:14:34.270
So it goes back to that idea of
this platform that we created.

00:14:34.270 --> 00:14:38.110
This platform for us created
a single source of truth.

00:14:38.110 --> 00:14:40.600
Many of you are using
multiple tools, very likely.

00:14:40.600 --> 00:14:43.210
And what we do is we have just
that single platform where all

00:14:43.210 --> 00:14:44.557
these signals come together.

00:14:44.557 --> 00:14:47.140
So you don't have to stitch and
cobble together multiple tools

00:14:47.140 --> 00:14:49.580
to try to create
that source of truth.

00:14:49.580 --> 00:14:51.880
So if you're a
DevOps or an SRE, you

00:14:51.880 --> 00:14:54.142
may want to look primarily
at your infrastructure.

00:14:54.142 --> 00:14:55.600
If you're a developer,
you may look

00:14:55.600 --> 00:14:57.820
at your application
and your workloads

00:14:57.820 --> 00:15:00.030
and so on, which we'll
show in the demo.

00:15:00.030 --> 00:15:02.230
And if you're a
security engineer,

00:15:02.230 --> 00:15:04.900
you may actually be looking
at your logs and audit logs

00:15:04.900 --> 00:15:06.340
and audit trail and so on.

00:15:06.340 --> 00:15:09.580
That's actually also
part of Stackdriver.

00:15:09.580 --> 00:15:12.520
So I mentioned before it works
very well with open source,

00:15:12.520 --> 00:15:13.550
with Prometheus.

00:15:13.550 --> 00:15:16.090
One of the main maintainers of
Prometheus, Fabian Reinartz,

00:15:16.090 --> 00:15:17.530
joined Google--

00:15:17.530 --> 00:15:20.180
our team-- just a
couple of months ago.

00:15:20.180 --> 00:15:21.640
So we're directly
working with him

00:15:21.640 --> 00:15:24.250
to make sure that we continue
to contribute to Prometheus,

00:15:24.250 --> 00:15:27.670
to expand and contribute
to this vibrant community,

00:15:27.670 --> 00:15:30.550
and ultimately to make it very
easy to switch and make sure

00:15:30.550 --> 00:15:33.490
that Prometheus integrations
can move into cloud when it's

00:15:33.490 --> 00:15:36.910
appropriate and when
wanted and needed.

00:15:36.910 --> 00:15:39.120
So this is what we'll
be seeing today--

00:15:39.120 --> 00:15:43.260
a hybrid, multi-cluster approach
for Kubernetes Monitoring.

00:15:43.260 --> 00:15:46.320
And I think the best way to
