00:28:27.461 --> 00:28:28.962
explorer.

00:28:28.962 --> 00:28:30.420
And with metric
explorers, you have

00:28:30.420 --> 00:28:33.660
access to all of the objects and
components of Kubernetes, all

00:28:33.660 --> 00:28:34.964
the metrics that are available.

00:28:34.964 --> 00:28:36.630
And as Shoucong is
showing us right now,

00:28:36.630 --> 00:28:39.960
we have all the Prometheus
metrics ingested already.

00:28:39.960 --> 00:28:43.970
So take a look at one of the
JVM metrics that we have.

00:28:43.970 --> 00:28:48.395
This is a Java application,
so we have one of them.

00:28:48.395 --> 00:28:51.020
We can do the memory used bytes,
have some interesting signals,

00:28:51.020 --> 00:28:53.490
Shoucong, that we can show.

00:28:53.490 --> 00:28:55.840
So we've got autocompletion
will get you to the metrics

00:28:55.840 --> 00:28:57.370
that you need very fast.

00:28:57.370 --> 00:28:59.920
And then use bytes.

00:28:59.920 --> 00:29:02.310
So we have a lot of
signals in that sense.

00:29:02.310 --> 00:29:06.490
It may be hard to figure
out exactly what's going on.

00:29:06.490 --> 00:29:08.890
But what we can do is we
added a lot of dimensions

00:29:08.890 --> 00:29:10.660
to these Kubernetes objects.

00:29:10.660 --> 00:29:13.499
And so now you have
labels and metadata

00:29:13.499 --> 00:29:15.040
that are coming from
your environment

00:29:15.040 --> 00:29:17.230
or even from user-defined
labels that you

00:29:17.230 --> 00:29:21.220
can use to filter and aggregate
and group by to get you right,

00:29:21.220 --> 00:29:23.050
very fast, to where you need.

00:29:23.050 --> 00:29:26.290
And so let's filter now by
service name as you're doing.

00:29:26.290 --> 00:29:28.740
And we knew before there was
something with API Gateway

00:29:28.740 --> 00:29:29.240
above.

00:29:29.240 --> 00:29:33.340
So let's filter by that one.

00:29:33.340 --> 00:29:36.220
And then you can further group
by something else or aggregate

00:29:36.220 --> 00:29:37.750
to not just have
a sum but rather

00:29:37.750 --> 00:29:40.630
perhaps a mean or
a 99th percentile

00:29:40.630 --> 00:29:41.915
or something of the sort.

00:29:41.915 --> 00:29:43.540
And then the last
thing that you can do

00:29:43.540 --> 00:29:45.426
is you also do outlier mode.

00:29:45.426 --> 00:29:47.050
So we still have a
lot of signals here.

00:29:47.050 --> 00:29:49.780
So you can look at
the top end objects.

00:29:49.780 --> 00:29:52.160
And so we're now looking
at the top three,

00:29:52.160 --> 00:29:58.460
top five containers by
JVM memory used bytes.

00:29:58.460 --> 00:30:00.100
So that gives you
just a whirlwind tour

00:30:00.100 --> 00:30:03.040
of the main things that you
can do on the monitoring

00:30:03.040 --> 00:30:06.890
piece with the integration of
metrics, logs, and so forth.

00:30:06.890 --> 00:30:08.479
So I'm going to
switch with Shoucong

00:30:08.479 --> 00:30:11.020
so we can look at more of the
troubleshooting scenario that's

00:30:11.020 --> 00:30:12.670
started to occur right now.

00:30:12.670 --> 00:30:13.420
SHOUCONG CHEN: OK.

00:30:13.420 --> 00:30:15.940
So I'm going to give a demo
about a troubleshooting

00:30:15.940 --> 00:30:16.960
scenario.

00:30:16.960 --> 00:30:19.990
So as a software engineer
or operation engineer,

00:30:19.990 --> 00:30:22.610
one of the very common
error case that will happen

00:30:22.610 --> 00:30:24.490
and we'll deal
with in daily work

00:30:24.490 --> 00:30:27.280
is when your
application is receiving

00:30:27.280 --> 00:30:28.690
a large volume of traffic.

00:30:28.690 --> 00:30:32.350
Your application may become
less responsive or slow.

00:30:32.350 --> 00:30:35.410
And so this is what we have
simulated a moment ago,

00:30:35.410 --> 00:30:36.770
if you'll recall.

00:30:36.770 --> 00:30:39.530
While JD is speaking,
we generate traffic

00:30:39.530 --> 00:30:44.610
to both Kubernetes engine
cluster and on-prem cluster.

00:30:44.610 --> 00:30:47.630
And while you were
watching the previous demo,

00:30:47.630 --> 00:30:49.630
[INAUDIBLE] as the
[? encore ?] engineer

00:30:49.630 --> 00:30:53.210
has been paged by incident.

00:30:53.210 --> 00:30:56.270
So let's go to my Gmail account.

00:30:56.270 --> 00:30:59.980
And I see there already
are two incident reports

00:30:59.980 --> 00:31:01.980
have been sent again to me.

00:31:01.980 --> 00:31:05.830
For example, this
incident tells me there

00:31:05.830 --> 00:31:09.270
are too many 500 has been
returned from my services.

00:31:09.270 --> 00:31:11.480
And on the bottom of
this incident report,

00:31:11.480 --> 00:31:13.720
there's a View
Incident link, which

00:31:13.720 --> 00:31:18.010
will redirect me to the
Stackdriver incident overview

00:31:18.010 --> 00:31:19.430
page.

00:31:19.430 --> 00:31:22.450
So as you can see
here, so my service

00:31:22.450 --> 00:31:27.000
is apparently returning
a lot of 500 response.

00:31:27.000 --> 00:31:29.740
And this is concerning
because this is how

00:31:29.740 --> 00:31:31.640
my customers are experiencing.

00:31:31.640 --> 00:31:37.600
And this is too many 500s,
more than my SOO allows.

00:31:37.600 --> 00:31:41.880
So I want to resolve this
issue as soon as possible.

00:31:41.880 --> 00:31:44.050
Let's move to the
custom dashboard

00:31:44.050 --> 00:31:49.390
I have previously built
for my hybrid environments.

00:31:49.390 --> 00:31:51.450
So this is a custom
dashboard I built

00:31:51.450 --> 00:31:55.240
before there to draw
several charts about very

00:31:55.240 --> 00:31:59.560
useful metrics to help me
observe my hybrid environments.

00:31:59.560 --> 00:32:01.120
Let's quickly go through them.

00:32:01.120 --> 00:32:05.640
So on the first row, there are
two charts about my activity

00:32:05.640 --> 00:32:08.500
request rate and error rate.

00:32:08.500 --> 00:32:13.430
This is [? to ?]
[? slice ?] by the cluster.

00:32:13.430 --> 00:32:16.180
And on the second row,
there are two JVM metrics

00:32:16.180 --> 00:32:19.540
because my PetClinic app
is a Java application.

00:32:19.540 --> 00:32:22.780
So JVM metrics that were
important for me and this

00:32:22.780 --> 00:32:25.320
is corrected by Prometheus.

00:32:25.320 --> 00:32:27.470
And it's installed
in Stackdriver.

00:32:27.470 --> 00:32:30.910
I also have the CPU limit
utilization and memory limit

00:32:30.910 --> 00:32:32.820
utilization and [INAUDIBLE].

00:32:32.820 --> 00:32:35.440
Some are classical
system metrics.

00:32:35.440 --> 00:32:37.690
I will take a look.

00:32:37.690 --> 00:32:41.290
So this is a at-a-glance
view of this dashboard.

00:32:41.290 --> 00:32:43.440
Let's see how we can use it.

00:32:43.440 --> 00:32:45.160
So from the first
chart, we already

00:32:45.160 --> 00:32:50.110
see there's a reasonable amount,
about 300 requests per second,

00:32:50.110 --> 00:32:54.160
goes to both my on-prem cluster
and Kubernetes engine cluster.

00:32:54.160 --> 00:32:56.310
This is working as
intended because this is

00:32:56.310 --> 00:32:59.470
one my load balancer can fix.

00:32:59.470 --> 00:33:03.130
But if we look at a second
chart, which is my error rates,

00:33:03.130 --> 00:33:06.570
I can see that's about
all of the 500 rates

00:33:06.570 --> 00:33:09.370
are coming from my
on-prem cluster.

00:33:09.370 --> 00:33:11.620
So it's a permanent,
very strong signal

00:33:11.620 --> 00:33:15.310
to tell me there's something
wrong in my on-prem cluster.

00:33:15.310 --> 00:33:17.380
As a natural reaction,
I may want to dive

00:33:17.380 --> 00:33:20.470
into my on-prem cluster and
figure out what's wrong there.

00:33:20.470 --> 00:33:24.660
But our SI best practice tell
us we should [? compare ?]

00:33:24.660 --> 00:33:28.000
and mitigate the issue first.

00:33:28.000 --> 00:33:32.140
So the ideal solution here is
we drain the on-prem cluster

00:33:32.140 --> 00:33:34.930
and failover its traffic
to somewhere else.

00:33:34.930 --> 00:33:38.770
The first choice is our
Kubernetes engine closer.

00:33:38.770 --> 00:33:42.440
But before I do that, I want to
ensure the additional safety.

00:33:42.440 --> 00:33:45.760
I want make sure my Kubernetes
engine cluster can sustain

00:33:45.760 --> 00:33:48.480
the load if I double the size.

00:33:48.480 --> 00:33:51.900
So let's go to the
Kubernetes monitoring UI

00:33:51.900 --> 00:33:54.710
and take a look at the
infrastructure view.

00:33:54.710 --> 00:34:00.210
If we expand the
Kubernetes engine cluster,

00:34:00.210 --> 00:34:02.400
we will see there
are six nodes there.

00:34:02.400 --> 00:34:05.580
Some of the nodes
are reasonably busy.

00:34:05.580 --> 00:34:07.630
But most of them are empty.

00:34:07.630 --> 00:34:10.679
So it gives me confidence
I can failover the traffic

00:34:10.679 --> 00:34:12.389
into this Kubernetes
engine cluster,

00:34:12.389 --> 00:34:15.070
and it's more than enough
to sustain the load.

00:34:15.070 --> 00:34:18.440
So let's go ahead and do it.

00:34:18.440 --> 00:34:22.760
So from this terminal, what
JD is doing is to scale down,

00:34:22.760 --> 00:34:25.060
manually drain the
on-prem cluster

00:34:25.060 --> 00:34:27.670
by scaling the traffic down.

00:34:27.670 --> 00:34:30.010
In the same time, we
will go to a Cloud

00:34:30.010 --> 00:34:31.570
SQL for Kubernetes engine.

