00:46:32.290 --> 00:46:35.270
JD VALASQUEZ: So as Shoucong
showed, the idea here, again,

00:46:35.270 --> 00:46:38.370
is you have many tools
available from monitoring

00:46:38.370 --> 00:46:39.852
to logging to debugger.

00:46:39.852 --> 00:46:41.560
We didn't show you
error reporting today.

00:46:41.560 --> 00:46:42.910
But there's a few errors
that you would see.

00:46:42.910 --> 00:46:45.320
And we categorized those
errors directly for you.

00:46:45.320 --> 00:46:48.061
So it's easy to do
exception analysis as well.

00:46:48.061 --> 00:46:49.810
And when you use all
those tools together,

00:46:49.810 --> 00:46:51.940
you get very rich,
comprehensive observability

00:46:51.940 --> 00:46:53.530
of your Kubernetes environment.

00:46:53.530 --> 00:46:55.690
Now, you can help with
multiple things, of course.

00:46:55.690 --> 00:46:57.550
You can increase that
observability for making sure

00:46:57.550 --> 00:46:59.980
that you get out-of-the-box
metrics that your Kubernetes

00:46:59.980 --> 00:47:01.550
environment gives you.

00:47:01.550 --> 00:47:03.220
And so we do that for you.

00:47:03.220 --> 00:47:06.430
We take that toil away
from you to make it easy,

00:47:06.430 --> 00:47:08.530
very much in the way that
Google does internally.

00:47:08.530 --> 00:47:11.710
And you certainly can supplement
with third-party application

00:47:11.710 --> 00:47:13.539
metrics and your
business-defined metrics

00:47:13.539 --> 00:47:15.580
for which you can use
Prometheus or anything else

00:47:15.580 --> 00:47:17.350
that you may want
for instrumentation,

00:47:17.350 --> 00:47:19.569
hopefully using OpenCensus
so you can do it just once

00:47:19.569 --> 00:47:21.360
and then choose any
other back end that you

00:47:21.360 --> 00:47:23.380
may be interested in.

00:47:23.380 --> 00:47:27.550
Make sure that these tools pay
attention to resource requests

00:47:27.550 --> 00:47:28.490
and limits.

00:47:28.490 --> 00:47:31.450
And so on some of it, the tools
that we have, on some place

00:47:31.450 --> 00:47:33.100
it would show you
no data available

00:47:33.100 --> 00:47:36.650
or no request or resource
request or limit set.

00:47:36.650 --> 00:47:38.609
It is important, that's
a good best practice,

00:47:38.609 --> 00:47:40.900
that you use that and set
your resource requests limits

00:47:40.900 --> 00:47:42.257
in your Kubernetes environment.

00:47:42.257 --> 00:47:44.590
And also, you want to make
sure that your information is

00:47:44.590 --> 00:47:47.020
available in a consistent
way throughout.

00:47:47.020 --> 00:47:49.510
So take advantage of
the Kubernetes labels.

00:47:49.510 --> 00:47:51.630
That's the way in which
we organize information

00:47:51.630 --> 00:47:52.250
all together.

00:47:52.250 --> 00:47:55.820
So from your user-defined
labels to anything else,

00:47:55.820 --> 00:47:58.540
so you can keep track of where
your application is no matter

00:47:58.540 --> 00:48:01.870
where it's running in the many
different possible Kubernetes

00:48:01.870 --> 00:48:03.740
components that may
be [? rescheduled. ?]

00:48:03.740 --> 00:48:06.470
So we're now at the
end of the talk.

